From: OpenWrt HA build <local>
Subject: [PATCH] owsync: accept only the configured peers and cap frames before the handshake

The sync port is reachable by every host that can route to the node.
owsync 1.3.0 bounds what a connected client can do (socket timeouts,
aggregate receive budget, explicit-length JSON parsing, mode masking,
sync root boundary), but any host can still connect, hold a worker slot
for the socket timeout and make the server read a MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE frame
before the AES-GCM tag is checked.

- accept only connections from the addresses of the configured peers
  (standalone "owsync server" without peers keeps accepting everyone);
- frames are capped at 64 KiB until a HELLO has been accepted on the
  connection. This is not proof of the key, since the server's own HELLO
  sent back to it passes, but a client that sends nothing valid can no
  longer make the server read a large frame;
- a FileContent frame without path, without data or with data that is
  not hex is malformed: written as received, it would replace the
  destination with an empty file.

The parts of the 1.2.0 version of this patch that 1.3.0 covers are
dropped: the socket timeouts (300 s upstream, sized for the peer's scan
of its tree, instead of 30 s), the NUL terminators (wire buffers are now
parsed with their length), the empty frame check, the sync root boundary
check and the fchmod() mask.

The two-node exchange is unchanged: both peers are configured on each
side and HELLO stays well below 64 KiB.

--- a/include/net.h
+++ b/include/net.h
@@ -47,10 +47,19 @@ typedef struct {
     } payload;
 } message_t;
 
+/* Largest frame accepted before a HELLO has been accepted on the channel.
+ * A HELLO is a few hundred bytes; the full MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE is only
+ * granted after one. This keeps a client that sends nothing valid from
+ * making the server read large frames. It is not proof of the key: the
+ * server speaks first, and its own HELLO sent back passes. The accept
+ * filter and the aggregate receive budget bound what remains. */
+#define OWSYNC_PREAUTH_MAX_MESSAGE (64 * 1024)
+
 /* TCP channel with optional encryption */
 typedef struct {
     int fd;                /* Socket file descriptor */
     bool is_encrypted;     /* True if using AES-256-GCM encryption */
+    uint32_t max_message;  /* Largest frame accepted on this channel right now */
 #ifdef ENABLE_ENCRYPTION
     security_context_t *security_ctx;  /* Encryption context (NULL if plain) */
 #endif
@@ -66,6 +75,12 @@ void channel_free(channel_t *channel);  /* Closes socket */
 int channel_send(channel_t *channel, message_t *msg);
 int channel_receive(channel_t *channel, message_t **out_msg);
 
+/* Restrict the server to connections from the configured peers.
+ * peers use the "addr[,source_addr]" format of the daemon configuration;
+ * every address a peer name resolves to is accepted. With count == 0 the
+ * server accepts any client (standalone "owsync server" mode). */
+void net_set_allowed_peers(char **peers, size_t count);
+
 /* Server mode: listen for incoming connections, spawn threads */
 int start_server(const char *host, const char *port, const char *key,
                  const char *root, const char *db_path, file_filter_t *filter,
--- a/src/daemon.c
+++ b/src/daemon.c
@@ -280,6 +280,9 @@ static void *server_thread(void *arg) {
 
     log_info("Starting listen server on %s:%s", ctx->host, ctx->port);
 
+    /* Only the configured peers may open sync sessions */
+    net_set_allowed_peers(ctx->peers, ctx->peer_count);
+
     /* start_server runs until process termination */
     int result = start_server(ctx->host, ctx->port, ctx->key, ctx->root,
                               ctx->db_path, ctx->filter, ctx->clock_offset);
